1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,217
Slide 14 illustrates RESOURCE COLLISION: 8GB VS.

2
00:00:05,217 --> 00:00:11,510
32GB MACHINES. James, what happens to normal corporate laptops?

3
00:00:11,910 --> 00:00:19,521
In Silicon Valley, all the software architects have 64GB MacBook Pros, so they think 1.2GB is nothing!

4
00:00:19,521 --> 00:00:26,910
But in the real enterprise world, 70% of corporate employees are issued 8GB Dell or Lenovo laptops!

5
00:00:27,310 --> 00:00:34,378
Look at the math on the left card: Windows 11 takes 3.5GB of RAM.

6
00:00:34,378 --> 00:00:37,422
Microsoft Teams takes 1.2GB.

7
00:00:37,422 --> 00:00:39,596
Outlook takes 800MB.

8
00:00:39,596 --> 00:00:43,730
That is already 5.5GB used out of 8GB!

9
00:00:44,130 --> 00:00:49,701
Now install the 1.2GB Google App—you are at 6.7GB, which is 84% memory load!

10
00:00:49,701 --> 00:01:00,110
The second the user opens a 20MB Excel spreadsheet, Windows runs out of physical RAM and starts aggressively swapping memory pages to the SSD!

11
00:01:00,510 --> 00:01:06,990
The laptop fans scream, the mouse cursor stutters, and the employee thinks their computer has a virus!

12
00:01:07,390 --> 00:01:16,990
Meanwhile, on the right card, the developer on a 32GB rig says: Works fine on my machine! That is the ultimate developer disconnect!

13
00:01:17,390 --> 00:01:23,270
Empathy for the end-user's hardware constraints is the mark of a mature intelligence architect.

